# run-benchmark-pro.ps1.example
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEMPLATE -- copy this file to "run-benchmark-pro.ps1" (no ".example") in
# the same folder and fill in your own values. run-benchmark-pro.ps1 is
# gitignored on purpose: it hardcodes a personal account/config-dir path,
# so each person on this project needs their own copy, not a shared one.
#
# What this is for: routing this project's `claude -p` calls through a
# SEPARATE Claude account (e.g. a company Team/Enterprise seat) instead of
# whichever account your default `claude` CLI is already logged into --
# so this project's token usage doesn't eat into your personal account's
# quota/limits, and you never have to manually log out/in to switch.
#
# How it works: the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR environment variable tells the
# `claude` CLI where to read/write its OAuth credentials. Setting it here,
# scoped to this script's own process, only affects `claude` calls made
# downstream by run-benchmark.ps1 in THIS run. It does NOT touch Claude
# Desktop, claude.ai in a browser, or any other terminal/session on your
# machine -- those keep using whatever they're already logged into,
# completely unaffected.
#
# ONE-TIME SETUP (do this yourself, once, in your own PowerShell -- OAuth
# needs a real browser window, this can't be scripted):
#
#   $env:CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR = "C:\Users\<you>\.claude-pro"   # pick any path
#   claude
#   /login
#   # choose your separate account (e.g. "Claude for Teams or Enterprise"),
#   # sign in in the browser, then exit once logged in -- the token persists
#   # on disk at that path from then on, no need to repeat this.
#
# After that, run-benchmark-nightly.ps1 automatically picks up and uses
# this file if it exists next to it (see the check near the top of that
# script) -- nothing else to wire up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$env:CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR = "C:\Users\<you>\.claude-pro"
& "$PSScriptRoot\run-benchmark.ps1" @args
